<section>
  <h2>Explanation</h2>
  <p>
    A two-particle pulley joins two masses by a light inextensible string over
    a smooth pulley: one hangs, one sits on an incline. They share one
    acceleration <i>a</i> and one tension <i>T</i>. Resolve along the string,
    include friction <i>μN</i> opposite <i>m</i>₁'s motion, then solve for
    <i>a</i> and <i>T</i> — <i>μ</i> = 0 is the smooth case.
  </p>
  <p>
    The string is inextensible, so the accelerations have equal magnitude.
    The pulley is smooth, so <i>T</i> is the same throughout.
  </p>
  <p>
    The normal reaction on <i>m</i>₁ is <i>N</i> = <i>m</i>₁<i>g</i> cos θ, so
    the friction force has magnitude <i>F</i> = <i>μN</i> = <i>μ m</i>₁<i>g</i> cos θ.
    Friction opposes the motion of <i>m</i>₁ along the plane.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If <i>m</i>₂<i>g</i> &gt; <i>m</i>₁<i>g</i> sin θ, <i>m</i>₂ tends to descend and <i>m</i>₁ goes up the slope, so friction acts down the slope.</li>
    <li>If <i>m</i>₁<i>g</i> sin θ &gt; <i>m</i>₂<i>g</i>, <i>m</i>₁ tends to slide down, so friction acts up the slope.</li>
    <li>If the driving difference is at most <i>F</i>, the system stays at rest and <i>T</i> = <i>m</i>₂<i>g</i>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Taking downward on <i>m</i>₂ and up the slope on <i>m</i>₁ as positive,
    and assuming <i>m</i>₂ descends:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>For <i>m</i>₂: <i>m</i>₂<i>g</i> − <i>T</i> = <i>m</i>₂<i>a</i></li>
    <li>For <i>m</i>₁: <i>T</i> − <i>m</i>₁<i>g</i> sin θ − <i>μ m</i>₁<i>g</i> cos θ = <i>m</i>₁<i>a</i></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Add the equations to eliminate <i>T</i> and solve for <i>a</i>, then
    substitute back for <i>T</i>. If <i>μ</i> = 0 the friction terms vanish.
  </p>
</section>
